--- ANSWER ---
Yes. The premise that there is no programmatic Error List accessor is incorrect: LabVIEW has a hidden/private VI method specifically for this.

## 1. Programmatic Error List

### VI class: `Get Errors`

- Long/data name: `Get Errors`
- Method ID: `0x452`
- Owning class: VI, class ID `2`
- Scope: Private, also categorized as a VI Scripting method
- Outputs/parameters: `Errors`, `Details`, and optional `Call Dangerously?`
- Availability: LabVIEW development environment; the class table marks it as available through VI Server, but it is unsupported/private.

The parameter metadata is incomplete publicly, but both `Errors` and `Details` are required outputs. [LabVIEW Wiki method entry](https://labviewwiki.org/wiki/VI_class/Get_Errors_method)

This is the method you want. An NI Community example reports that it returns all errors from a broken scripted VI, although the returned representation/text differs slightly from the interactive Error List. The original poster confirmed it was usable for exactly this purpose. [NI Community: Error list in VI scripting](https://forums.ni.com/t5/LabVIEW/Error-list-in-VI-scripting/td-p/2263216/highlight/true)

It may not appear in the Invoke Node menu even when ordinary VI Scripting is enabled. The forum thread recommends these INI tokens:

```ini
SuperPrivateScriptingFeatureVisible=True
SuperSecretPrivateSpecialStuff=True
SuperPrivateSpecialStuff=True
```

The method can also be copied from an existing VI even when it remains absent from the menu. [NI Community accepted solution and follow-up](https://forums.ni.com/t5/LabVIEW/Error-list-in-VI-scripting/m-p/2268238)

Important distinction: `Get Errors` is a native VI Server Invoke Node method. I found no evidence that it is directly exported as an Automation method on the ActiveX VI object/type library. In your architecture, the reliable route is therefore an Op VI containing the native Invoke Node, called through ActiveX?�not assuming Python can dispatch `vi.GetErrors()` directly.

There is also:

### Wire class: `Get Error List`

- Long name: `Get Error List`
- Data name: `GetErrorList`
- Method ID: `0x6370C0A`
- Owning class: Wire, class ID `16439`
- Scope: Private
- Required output: `Error List`

This returns errors associated with one wire, rather than the complete VI Error List. [LabVIEW Wiki](https://labviewwiki.org/wiki/Wire_class/Get_Error_List_method)

## 2. Indirect diagnostics

The useful scripting properties are narrower than the suggested names.

### Broken wires

`Wire.Is Broken?`

- Property ID: `0x6371004`
- Scope: VI Scripting
- Read-only Boolean
- Meaning: true if the wire is broken or otherwise in a bad state.

There is no documented `Wire.IsBad`; its actual public scripting name is `Is Broken?`. [Wire class table](https://labviewwiki.org/wiki/Wire_class)

Useful companions:

- `Wire.Terminals[]`, ID `0x6371003`, VI Scripting. Multiple source terminals themselves imply a broken wire. [LabVIEW Wiki](https://labviewwiki.org/wiki/Wire_class/Terminals%28%29_property)
- `Wire.Get Error List`, ID `0x6370C0A`, private, gives the wire-specific error list. [LabVIEW Wiki](https://labviewwiki.org/wiki/Wire_class/Get_Error_List_method)

I found no `Diagram.Bad Wires[]` property in the published class inventories. Enumerating diagram objects recursively and testing each Wire?�s `Is Broken?` is the supported scripting-style approach.

### Terminals

The Terminal class does not expose a type-conflict or ?�bad terminal??Boolean. Relevant VI Scripting properties are:

- `Connected Wire`, ID `0x634A000`
- `Type Descriptor`, ID `0x634A001`
- `Is Source?`, ID `0x634A003`
- `Name`, ID `0x634A004`
- `Coercion Dot?`, ID `0x634A006`
- `Type Descriptor (Full)`, ID `0x634A007`
- `Data Type`, ID `0x634A008`

Thus, a type conflict is best detected from the connected Wire?�s `Is Broken?` or `Get Error List`; terminal type descriptors can help explain the mismatch but do not themselves identify an error. [Terminal class table](https://labviewwiki.org/wiki/Terminal_class)

### SubVIs

The relevant properties are:

- `VI Reference`, ID `0x635E406`, VI Scripting, read-only.
- `Bad SubVI Linkage`, ID `0x635E40B`, VI Scripting, read-only; true when connector-pane changes require relinking.
- `Missing VI Path`, ID `0x635E40C`, VI Scripting, read-only.
- `Missing VI Name`, ID `0x635E40D`, VI Scripting, read-only.

There is no listed `Is Missing` property. A missing-call node can instead be recognized through `Missing VI Path`/`Missing VI Name` and failure to obtain a valid `VI Reference`. [SubVI class table](https://labviewwiki.org/wiki/SubVI_class)

### Generic nodes

I found no general `Node.Bad`, `Node.Is Bad?`, or equivalent property in the published Node-class inventory. `Node.Terminals[]`, ID `0x6359000`, provides the terminals for inspection, but actual compiler diagnostics should come from `VI.Get Errors` or the object-specific error-list methods. [Node.Terminals[]](https://www.labviewwiki.org/wiki/Node_class/Terminals%28%29_property)

## 3. Opening the Error List without Ctrl+L

Yes:

### Application class: `Error Window:Open`

- Method ID: `0x89D`
- Owning class: Application, class ID `1`
- Scope: Private
- Long name: `Error Window:Open`

It is the direct private method for opening the Error List/Error Window. [Application method table](https://labviewwiki.org/wiki/Application_class)

Related methods:

- `VI.Refresh Error Window Entry`, ID `0x473`, private. This appears intended to refresh that VI?�s entry before/while displaying the window. [VI class table](https://labviewwiki.org/wiki/VI_class)
- `Application.Error Window:Defer Updates`, ID `0x915`, private. [Application method table](https://labviewwiki.org/wiki/Application_class)

The likely screenshot sequence is:

1. Invoke `VI.Refresh Error Window Entry` on the broken VI.
2. Invoke `Application.Error Window:Open`.
3. Capture the Error List window.

Because these are private native methods, I would implement that sequence inside an Op VI. I found no reliable evidence that `Error Window:Open` is exposed directly through the LabVIEW ActiveX automation type library.

Bottom line: use VI private method `Get Errors`, ID `0x452`, for machine-readable diagnostics. Use Application private method `Error Window:Open`, ID `0x89D`, when you specifically need the UI for a screenshot.
